// THE IMAGE THAT DOES NOT EXIST YET, BUILT AS A MATERIALIZATION RATHER THAN RUN AS A SCRIPT.
//
// WHAT THIS LANE IS FOR. gunbc.spark.serving_runtime_capability_probe measured every published GB10
// container image against the five capabilities DeepSeek V4.1 needs and found none that registers
// DeepseekV41ForCausalLM on sm_121. Its conclusion was not "no image works" but a BUILD OBLIGATION:
// the only source known to contain the V4.1 path is vLLM at 07b75534651a0dd584c6f3be75d8c62fb8426397,
// and the way to settle what that source yields on compute capability 12.1 is to build it and ask the
// same questions inside the result. This module is that build.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT A SHELL SCRIPT SOMEBODY RAN. A `docker build` invocation typed into a terminal
// produces an image whose provenance is a memory: which base, which arch list, which revision. The
// DESIGN §6 tell for that is out-of-band actuation, and its cost here is concrete rather than
// stylistic -- the whole product of this lane is a DIGEST whose inputs someone else must be able to
// re-derive, and an image whose arch list nobody recorded is an image nobody can rebuild or refute.
// So the inputs are declarations, the identity of the build is a content hash OVER those
// declarations, and the invocation is derived from them through the same ArgvCommand wall every
// other tool invocation in this repository passes.
//
// THE PRINCIPAL IS THE MANAGED EXECUTOR, NOT THE OPERATOR, AND THAT WAS A SECOND LITERAL IN THE SAME
// PLACE AS THE FIRST. An earlier revision authenticated as gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap's
// OPERATOR bootstrap login -- the human account, which exists to take a PASSWORD on first contact and
// refuses key authentication by design on every Spark. It worked only on the hosts where a human had
// already installed a key by hand, which is precisely the provisioning state this lane should not be
// depending on. `spark_managed_executor_login` is the account the modeled bootstrap route converges,
// and the one the fleet key is laid for; naming it here is naming the authority rather than the
// account that happened to work.
//
// TWO TRANSPORTS, AND WHICH ONE A LEG TAKES IS DECIDED BY WHAT THE LEG NEEDS.
//
// The GIT legs run through gunbc.typed_argv_exec typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh: a
// portable-word-sealed argv over an SSH leg whose known-hosts anchor was materialized and read back
// and whose credential was verified against the declared fleet key before any host was contacted.
// They need no root, and the managed executor can perform them.
//
// THE DOCKER LEGS CANNOT GO THAT WAY, AND AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS PARAGRAPH CLAIMED THE OPPOSITE AS
// A MEASURED FACT. It read that no sudo appears in this module and that the build reaches docker
// through the operator's docker group membership. Both halves were wrong together: the legs
// authenticate as the MANAGED EXECUTOR, not the operator, and that account is in no docker group on
// any Spark -- which this lane's own sibling module records as its measured finding. The claim
// survived because the one build that ever ran took the socket on srv5, a host carrying a
// hand-installed key for the human briansrls AND that account in the docker group: provisioning drift
// no modeled route produces, standing in for a mechanism nobody had. The docker legs now bind to
// gunbc.spark.model_snapshot_materialize spark_remote_run, the single route the corpus models for
// that daemon, which is privileged by necessity rather than by preference.

// ── CONFORMANCE, MATERIALIZATION DOMAIN (DESIGN §3b): WHAT THIS INHABITS AND WHERE IT DIVERGES ──
//
// INHABITED. The identity of a build is minted by std.artifact_store artifact_realization_digest over
// a list of NAMED realization inputs -- the authority that already answers "a set of named inputs
// folded into one content identity". The tag is derived from that key and from nothing else.
//
// DIVERGES, WITH THE REASON. std.materialization_provider's ArtifactRequest is a CLOSED coproduct
// over three compiler requests (parse a module, typecheck a module, resolve a closure), each keyed by
// a source or closure digest and a DeclaredInput compiler. A container image built by a foreign
// toolchain on another machine is none of those, and widening that coproduct to admit it would make
// the compiler's own provider vocabulary carry a fleet concern -- the layer inversion §3 names, not a
// missing row. std.realization's Materialization (Recompute | Memoize | Share) likewise decides how a
// step INSIDE one program's demand graph is realized; it has no place to say which host ran a build or
// what digest came back. So this module consumes the keying authority and does NOT route through the
// provider or step-realization ones, deliberately.
//
// WHAT WOULD DISSOLVE THE DIVERGENCE. Not a new ArtifactRequest arm: an artifact-store-shaped surface
// whose subject is an EXTERNAL build -- inputs, a host, a produced digest -- which does not exist in
// std today and which one lane's container build is not enough evidence to design. The second
// external-build consumer is the trigger; until then this is a stated departure rather than a fork,
// because nothing here re-answers a question those two modules already answer.

// ── WHERE A BUILD MAY RUN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// NO HOST IS NAMED IN THIS MODULE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. An earlier revision carried a literal
// build host, and the host it carried was a member of the Spark serving pair -- policy standing in a
// literal, which is the §3 tell exactly: "where may this run" is a workflow decision, not a property
// of a vLLM build. The entry takes its host from the caller and admits it against a DERIVED
// admissibility fact, so the roster cannot go stale against the fleet it describes.
//
// ADMISSIBILITY IS A SUBTRACTION FROM THE MODELED FLEET, not a written list, AND THE SUBTRACTION IS
// NO LONGER THIS MODULE'S. It used to be: `spark_build_host_excluded` folded the unit roster against
// the actively-claimed serving groups and the undischarged llama.cpp RPC reservations right here, and
// `admissible_spark_build_hosts` filtered by it. That was correct and it was one consumer's private
// copy of a fleet-wide fact. When serving selection needed the same question -- which Sparks is
// nobody else already using -- the choice was a second derivation or one authority, and two
// derivations of that differing by two hosts is the §3 fork that is invisible precisely because each
// side is locally right. So the fold moved to gunbc.spark.host_commitment at its root and this lane
// consumes it; the reasoning it was carrying moved with it, including the correction the witness
// caught here (a FabricGroup is a 400G cage of four hosts, so subtracting every group subtracts the
// fleet -- a cage is where a group COULD serve, spark_pair_serving_groups is where one DOES).
//
// WHAT THIS LANE STILL OWNS IS THE POLICY, and it is one sentence: a build may run on a host nothing
// else has claimed. `spark_host_is_uncommitted` is that predicate, and no name for it is minted here
// -- an `admissible_spark_build_hosts` alias standing beside `spark_uncommitted_hosts` would be one
// concept under two names, which is the other half of the same §3 law.

// A HOST THE CALLER NAMED IS NOT YET A HOST THIS LANE MAY USE, AND THE SEAM THAT DECIDES THAT IS NOT
// THIS MODULE'S ANY MORE.
//
// `SparkBuildHostAdmission` / `admit_spark_build_host` stood here and were called by exactly ONE of
// this module's two effectful entries; the other repeated the check as a bare boolean with its own
// refusal sentence, as did the published-image probe. One typed seam and two open-coded copies of it
// is the same fork this PR removed from the host set itself, one level up, and it had the sharper
// consequence: the witness exercised the typed one, so a regression in either copy left the suite
// green while an effect ran on a claimed host. The seam now belongs to gunbc.spark.host_commitment,
// which owns the fact it consults, and all three roots call it.
//
// WHAT THIS LANE STILL OWNS IS THE PURPOSE STRING it hands that seam -- what the host is wanted FOR,
// which is the one thing the commitment authority cannot know and the refusal has to say.
data vllm_build_host_purpose: NonEmptyStr = "a vLLM source build, which occupies the host for the better part of an hour" as NonEmptyStr

// THE BUILD ROOT IS DERIVED FROM THE PRINCIPAL THAT OWNS IT, NOT SPELLED. An earlier revision put it
// under the OPERATOR's home while the legs authenticate as the managed executor -- a path belonging to
// one account written into work performed by another, which is a permission error waiting at the far
// side and reads as a broken build rather than as the wrong account being named.
// gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap already derives a principal's home for exactly this reason.
data vllm_build_root_name: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc-vllm-build" as NonEmptyStr

fn vllm_build_root() -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([spark_managed_principal_home(login: spark_managed_executor_login as NonEmptyStr) as String, "/", vllm_build_root_name as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn vllm_source_dir() -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([vllm_build_root() as String, "/src"], "") as NonEmptyStr
}
data vllm_source_remote_name: NonEmptyStr = "origin" as NonEmptyStr
data vllm_image_repository: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc-vllm-dsv41-gb10" as NonEmptyStr

// ── WHAT IS BUILT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE FLEET RECIPE IS UPSTREAM'S OWN aarch64 RELEASE RECIPE WITH ONE FIELD CHANGED, and it is written
// that way -- reading each unchanged field off that row rather than re-spelling it -- so the single
// divergence is visible as a divergence. That field is the arch list: upstream's arm64 release builds
// 8.0 through 12.0, and GB10 is 12.1, which is why no published image can carry sm_121 kernels
// however recent its vLLM is.
//
// The parallelism fields are the other difference and they are deliberately NOT part of the build
// identity below; see the key.
data v41_gb10_torch_cuda_arch_list: List<NonEmptyStr> = ["12.1" as NonEmptyStr]

// HOW MUCH OF A HOST A BUILD MAY TAKE IS A POLICY ROW, NOT A MEASUREMENT, and an earlier version of
// this annotation transcribed one. It carried the build host's core count and free memory and
// upstream's documented figure for a different machine, three numbers copied into prose with no
// producer named -- which is the §6 tell exactly, and they rot the moment any of the three changes
// with nobody touching this file.
//
// WHAT IS CITEABLE IS UPSTREAM'S OWN RECIPE, and it is already a declaration:
// extdeps.vllm.source_build's aarch64 release row carries the parallelism upstream passes on a
// machine dedicated to the build. The rows below are this fleet's DEPARTURE from it, chosen low
// because a Spark is not a dedicated builder -- what shares the host is the fleet's business, not
// upstream's -- and they are inputs to a policy rather than readings of anything.
//
// WHAT WOULD RE-DERIVE THE HOST FACTS, if a reader wants them: spark_build_host_reachability_wet
// reaches an admissible host over the modeled route, and a capacity question belongs there as a
// second leg rather than as a sentence here.
data v41_build_max_jobs: Nat = 6

// nvcc_threads COUNTS HARDWARE THREADS, and this row is the fleet's own policy value rather than a
// restatement of upstream's ARG -- so it carries the unit std.measure already models, the same way
// extdeps.ci_runner's published runner sizes carry it for vcpus. The bare Nat stays on
// VllmContainerBuildRecipe, where the field IS upstream's ARG at the cited-spec boundary; the
// conversion happens once, at the call that builds the recipe.
data v41_build_nvcc_threads: HardwareThreadCount = hardware_thread_count(count: 2)

fn v41_gb10_recipe() -> VllmContainerBuildRecipe {
  vllm_recipe_targeting(
    base: vllm_07b75534_aarch64_release_recipe,
    torch_cuda_arch_list: v41_gb10_torch_cuda_arch_list,
    max_jobs: v41_build_max_jobs,
    nvcc_threads: hardware_thread_count_value(t: v41_build_nvcc_threads),
  )
}

fn vllm_recipe_arch_is_buildable(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> Bool {
  fold(r.torch_cuda_arch_list, init: true, f: (acc, a) => acc && vllm_cuda_arch_supported(arch: a))
}

// ── THE IDENTITY OF THE BUILD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A CONTENT KEY OVER THE INPUTS, AND THE TAG IS THAT KEY. The image this lane produces is a
// materialization: given the same source revision, the same bases, the same toolchain pins and the
// same arch list, it is the same artifact, and given a different one it is a different artifact that
// must not be able to wear the same name. So the tag is DERIVED from the key rather than chosen, and
// a reader who wants to know whether two images were built from the same inputs compares two strings
// instead of reading two build logs.
//
// WHAT THE KEY IS OVER, STATED SO IT IS NOT MISREAD. It is over the REQUEST -- the inputs as
// declared -- and two of those inputs are MUTABLE REFERENCES rather than content identities:
// BUILD_BASE_IMAGE and FINAL_BASE_IMAGE are registry TAGS, and a tag can be repointed at different
// bytes without the string changing. So two builds sharing a key are two builds that asked for the
// same thing, which is weaker than two builds that consumed the same bytes.
//
// 🟡 dissolve-on: resolve both base references to their manifest digests through the registry API at
// build time and key on those instead of on the tags, which makes the key a content identity end to
// end. The resolution is a plain registry lookup -- this lane already performs one for the published
// image -- so the trigger is a modeled registry-resolution operation, not new information.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE KEY. max_jobs and nvcc_threads are how much of the host the build
// was allowed to use, not what it produced; folding them in would mint a fresh identity every time
// the machine's spare capacity changed, and each fresh identity is a cache miss and a rebuild of
// something already built. That is the redundancy DESIGN §2 prices, bought for nothing: the compiler
// output does not depend on how many compilers ran at once.

fn vllm_image_build_key_inputs(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> List<ArtifactRealizationInput> {
  [
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "source_repository" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.source.repository) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "source_revision" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.source.full_revision) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "dockerfile_path" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.dockerfile_path) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "build_target" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.build_target) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "cuda_version" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.cuda_version) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "build_base_image" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.build_base_image) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "final_base_image" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.final_base_image) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "python_version" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.python_version) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "torch_version" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.torch_version) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "flashinfer_version" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.flashinfer_version) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "torch_cuda_arch_list" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: vllm_torch_cuda_arch_list_wire(archs: r.torch_cuda_arch_list) as NonEmptyStr) },
  ]
}

// THE KEY IS MINTED BY std.artifact_store, NOT BY A FOLD WRITTEN HERE. An earlier revision combined
// the input atoms with its own content_hash fold, which is a second spelling of
// artifact_realization_digest -- the authority that already exists for "a list of named realization
// inputs, folded into one content identity". Inhabiting it also buys the thing the hand fold did not
// have: every input carries its NAME, so two recipes that differ only by which field holds a value
// cannot collide, and a reader sees which inputs the key covers without counting positions.
fn vllm_image_build_key(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> ContentHash {
  artifact_realization_digest(inputs: vllm_image_build_key_inputs(r: r))
}

fn vllm_image_tag(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([vllm_image_repository as String, ":", serialize_content_hash(hash: vllm_image_build_key(r: r)) as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// ── THE INVOCATION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE BUILD ARGUMENTS ARE THE ONES UPSTREAM'S Dockerfile PUBLISHES, spelled exactly as its ARGs
// spell them. FINAL_BASE_IMAGE is not passed and that is deliberate: its ARG default is
// nvidia/cuda:${CUDA_VERSION}-base-ubuntu24.04, so passing CUDA_VERSION already determines it, and
// the recipe row records the value it resolves to. RUN_WHEEL_CHECK disables only upstream's PyPI
// publication-size guard, which is a rule about what may be uploaded to PyPI and has nothing to say
// about an image nobody publishes.
fn vllm_image_build_args(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> List<DockerBuildArg> {
  [
    DockerBuildArg { name: "CUDA_VERSION" as NonEmptyStr, value: r.cuda_version as String },
    DockerBuildArg { name: "BUILD_BASE_IMAGE" as NonEmptyStr, value: r.build_base_image as String },
    DockerBuildArg { name: "torch_cuda_arch_list" as NonEmptyStr, value: vllm_torch_cuda_arch_list_wire(archs: r.torch_cuda_arch_list) },
    DockerBuildArg { name: "max_jobs" as NonEmptyStr, value: to_string(r.max_jobs) },
    DockerBuildArg { name: "nvcc_threads" as NonEmptyStr, value: to_string(r.nvcc_threads) },
    DockerBuildArg { name: "RUN_WHEEL_CHECK" as NonEmptyStr, value: "false" },
  ]
}

fn vllm_image_dockerfile_path(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([vllm_source_dir() as String, "/", r.dockerfile_path as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn vllm_image_build_spec(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> DockerBuildSpec {
  DockerBuildSpec {
    tag: vllm_image_tag(r: r),
    context_dir: vllm_source_dir() as FilePath,
    dockerfile: vllm_image_dockerfile_path(r: r),
    target: r.build_target,
    platform: "linux/arm64" as NonEmptyStr,
    build_args: vllm_image_build_args(r: r),
  }
}

// ── THE LEGS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

type ImageLegOutcome
  = ImageLegRan { stdout: String }
  | ImageLegFailed { step: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

// DOCKER DOES NOT GO OVER THIS LEG, AND AN EARLIER REVISION SENT IT THERE ANYWAY. The key-authenticated
// leg below runs as the managed executor, and this PR's own recorded facts say that account is in no
// docker group on any Spark -- so every docker leg it carried would have authenticated and then been
// refused at the socket. It did not, once, on srv5: that host has a hand-installed key for the human
// briansrls AND that account in the docker group, which is provisioning drift no modeled route
// produces. The fallback build ran because of that drift, and recording it is the point -- an
// unaccounted-for state was load-bearing for a whole lane.
//
// So the docker legs bind to gunbc.spark.model_snapshot_materialize spark_remote_run, the one route
// the corpus models for that daemon, and this leg keeps the git work, which needs no root.
func vllm_docker_leg(ctx: SparkMaterializeContext, step: NonEmptyStr, command: ArgvCommand) -> ImageLegOutcome
  uses net: Network
{
  match spark_remote_run(ctx: ctx, argv: argv_words(command: command)) {
    RemoteUnreachable { cause: why } => ImageLegFailed { step: step, cause: why }
    RemoteRan { exit_code: code, stdout: out, stderr: err } =>
      if code != 0 {
        ImageLegFailed { step: step, cause: join(["exit=", to_string(code), " stderr=", trim(s: err)], "") }
      } else {
        ImageLegRan { stdout: trim(s: out) }
      }
  }
}

fn vllm_image_leg(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, step: NonEmptyStr, command: ArgvCommand) -> ImageLegOutcome {
  match typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh(target: target, context: context, argv: argv_words(command: command)) {
    TypedArgvExecRefused { reason: why } => ImageLegFailed { step: step, cause: why }
    TypedArgvExecConverged { result: r } =>
      if r.success {
        ImageLegRan { stdout: trim(s: r.stdout) }
      } else {
        ImageLegFailed { step: step, cause: join(["exit=", to_string(r.exit_code), " stderr=", trim(s: r.stderr)], "") }
      }
  }
}

// SOURCE MATERIALIZATION IS CONVERGENT, AND THE READBACK COMES FIRST. A tree already sitting at the
// requested commit needs nothing done to it, and a clone into an existing directory is an error
// rather than a no-op -- so the question "is HEAD already this revision" is asked before anything is
// created. It is also the only honest completion test: a source directory that EXISTS says nothing
// about which commit is checked out in it.
type VllmSourceState
  = VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: NonEmptyStr, already_present: Bool }
  | VllmSourceRefused { step: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

// HEAD IS NOT THE TREE, AND A BUILD STAMPED WITH A REVISION IT DID NOT BUILD IS THE FABRICATION §5
// FORBIDS. `rev-parse HEAD` answers which commit is checked out; it says nothing about a modified
// working tree, so a context with local edits would produce an image whose recorded provenance names
// an unmodified upstream revision. The porcelain status is empty exactly when the tree matches, so
// this is a readback rather than a parse, and a dirty context REFUSES rather than building something
// no one can reproduce.
fn vllm_source_is_clean(stdout: String) -> Bool {
  trim(s: stdout) == ""
}

fn vllm_source_head(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext) -> String? {
  match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: "source-head" as NonEmptyStr, command: git_rev_parse_head_command(repository_dir: vllm_source_dir() as String)) {
    ImageLegFailed { step: _, cause: _ } => none
    ImageLegRan { stdout: head } => Present { value: head }
  }
}

fn vllm_materialize_source_steps(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> List<ArgvCommand> {
  [
    mkdir_parents_command_at(mkdir_binary: mkdir_path_resolved_program, path: vllm_build_root() as String),
    git_clone_shallow_command(repository: vllm_clone_url(source: r.source), destination: vllm_source_dir() as String),
    git_fetch_shallow_revision_command(repository_dir: vllm_source_dir() as String, remote: vllm_source_remote_name as String, revision: r.source.full_revision as String),
    git_checkout_revision_command(repository_dir: vllm_source_dir() as String, revision: r.source.full_revision as String),
  ]
}

fn vllm_image_run_steps(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, step: NonEmptyStr, commands: List<ArgvCommand>) -> ImageLegOutcome? {
  fold(commands, init: none, f: (acc, c) =>
    match acc {
      Present { value: failure } => Present { value: failure }
      Absent =>
        match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: step, command: c) {
          ImageLegRan { stdout: _ } => none
          ImageLegFailed { step: s, cause: why } => Present { value: ImageLegFailed { step: s, cause: why } }
        }
    })
}

func vllm_source_tree_clean(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext) -> VllmSourceState?
  uses net: Network
{
  match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: "source-clean" as NonEmptyStr, command: git_status_porcelain_command(repository_dir: vllm_source_dir() as String)) {
    ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } =>
      Present { value: VllmSourceRefused { step: st, cause: join(["the working-tree status could not be read, so nothing is known about whether the context matches its revision: ", why], "") } }
    ImageLegRan { stdout: status } =>
      if vllm_source_is_clean(stdout: status) {
        none
      } else {
        Present { value: VllmSourceRefused {
          step: "source-clean" as NonEmptyStr,
          cause: join(["the source tree at ", vllm_source_dir() as String, " has local modifications, so an image built from it would be stamped with a revision it was not built from:\n", trim(s: status)], ""),
        } }
      }
  }
}

func converge_vllm_source(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> VllmSourceState
  uses net: Network
{
  match vllm_source_head(target: target, context: context) {
    Present { value: head } =>
      if head != (r.source.full_revision as String) {
        VllmSourceRefused {
          step: "source-head" as NonEmptyStr,
          cause: join(["the source tree at ", vllm_source_dir() as String, " is checked out at ", head, " and this build wants ", r.source.full_revision as String, "; it is not moved from under a build that may be reading it"], ""),
        }
      } else {
        match vllm_source_tree_clean(target: target, context: context) {
          Present { value: refusal } => refusal
          Absent => VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: r.source.full_revision, already_present: true }
        }
      }
    Absent =>
      match vllm_image_run_steps(target: target, context: context, step: "source-materialize" as NonEmptyStr, commands: vllm_materialize_source_steps(r: r)) {
        Present { value: failure } =>
          match failure {
            ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } => VllmSourceRefused { step: st, cause: why }
            ImageLegRan { stdout: _ } => VllmSourceRefused { step: "source-materialize" as NonEmptyStr, cause: "unreachable: a successful leg is never recorded as a failure" }
          }
        Absent =>
          match vllm_source_head(target: target, context: context) {
            Absent => VllmSourceRefused { step: "source-readback" as NonEmptyStr, cause: "the tree was materialized and HEAD still could not be read" }
            Present { value: head } =>
              if head != (r.source.full_revision as String) {
                VllmSourceRefused { step: "source-readback" as NonEmptyStr, cause: join(["checkout reported success and HEAD reads ", head], "") }
              } else {
                match vllm_source_tree_clean(target: target, context: context) {
                  Present { value: refusal } => refusal
                  Absent => VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: r.source.full_revision, already_present: false }
                }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

// ── THE IMAGE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE IDENTITY READ BACK IS THE IMAGE CONFIG DIGEST, NOT A REGISTRY DIGEST, and the difference is
// stated rather than glossed. A registry digest is the digest of a manifest a registry accepted, and
// this image is deliberately not pushed anywhere; what `docker image inspect --format {{.Id}}`
// returns is the sha256 of the image configuration, which is a content identity of the built image on
// this host and is what a `docker run` can be pinned to here. Calling it a registry digest would
// claim a publication that did not happen.
// WHAT PRODUCED THESE BYTES IS NOT THE SAME QUESTION AS WHAT WE ASKED FOR, and an earlier revision
// answered the second while recording the first. `built_from` and `build_key` are copied from the
// REQUESTED recipe, so an image that merely carries the expected tag -- retagged by hand, left over
// from another run, built from a different context -- was returned as though this lane had built it
// from that revision. The tag is a name, and a name is not provenance.
//
// THE ADOPTED ARM IS FOR AN IMAGE THIS LANE BUILT AND FOUND AGAIN, NOT FOR A PUBLISHED ONE. A pull of
// somebody else's image is not a build with unestablished provenance; it is not a build at all, and
// an earlier revision minted one of these for it -- attaching a recipe and a build key to a pull, so
// the receipt printed a fallback key beside a published digest. That mint is deleted: the probe's
// outcome carries a reference and the digest the daemon reported, and nothing else.
//
// SO PROVENANCE IS ITS OWN FIELD AND IT HAS AN UNESTABLISHED ARM. A build this run performed and read
// back carries BuiltByThisRun and the recipe genuinely describes it. An image found already carrying
// the tag carries AdoptedUnderExpectedTag: the digest is real and the recipe is what WOULD have been
// built, and nothing here claims the two correspond. That is the same distinction
// extdeps.vllm.runtime_defaults draws with ImageSourceEquivalence, for the same reason -- an
// unestablished relation must not produce a positively stated fact.
type VllmImageProvenance
  = BuiltByThisRun
  | AdoptedUnderExpectedTag

fn vllm_image_provenance_wire(p: VllmImageProvenance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match p {
    BuiltByThisRun => "built-by-this-run" as NonEmptyStr
    AdoptedUnderExpectedTag => "adopted-under-expected-tag (source equality UNESTABLISHED)" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

type VllmImageIdentity sole_constructor {
  tag: NonEmptyStr
  local_config_digest: NonEmptyStr
  requested_from: VllmSourceRevision
  build_key: ContentHash
  provenance: VllmImageProvenance
}

type VllmImageBuild
  = VllmImageBuilt { identity: VllmImageIdentity, already_present: Bool }
  | VllmImageBuildRefused { step: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

func converge_vllm_image(ctx: SparkMaterializeContext, r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> VllmImageBuild
  uses net: Network
{
  let tag = vllm_image_tag(r: r)
  match vllm_docker_leg(ctx: ctx, step: "image-digest" as NonEmptyStr, command: docker_image_digest_command(image: tag)) {
    ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } =>
      VllmImageBuildRefused { step: st, cause: join(["the image query did not run, so nothing is known about whether the tag exists: ", why], "") }
    ImageLegRan { stdout: digest } =>
      if digest != "" {
        VllmImageBuilt {
          identity: VllmImageIdentity {
            tag: tag, local_config_digest: digest as NonEmptyStr, requested_from: r.source,
            build_key: vllm_image_build_key(r: r), provenance: AdoptedUnderExpectedTag,
          },
          already_present: true,
        }
      } else {
        match vllm_docker_leg(ctx: ctx, step: "image-build" as NonEmptyStr, command: docker_build_spec_command(spec: vllm_image_build_spec(r: r))) {
          ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } => VllmImageBuildRefused { step: st, cause: why }
          ImageLegRan { stdout: _ } =>
            match vllm_docker_leg(ctx: ctx, step: "image-readback" as NonEmptyStr, command: docker_image_digest_command(image: tag)) {
              ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } => VllmImageBuildRefused { step: st, cause: join(["the build reported success and the readback did not run: ", why], "") }
              ImageLegRan { stdout: readback } =>
                if readback == "" {
                  VllmImageBuildRefused { step: "image-readback" as NonEmptyStr, cause: "the build reported success and no image carries the tag it was asked to write" }
                } else {
                  VllmImageBuilt {
                    identity: VllmImageIdentity {
                      tag: tag, local_config_digest: readback as NonEmptyStr, requested_from: r.source,
                      build_key: vllm_image_build_key(r: r), provenance: BuiltByThisRun,
                    },
                    already_present: false,
                  }
                }
            }
        }
      }
  }
}

// ── THE ENTRY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

data vllm_image_build_attempt_raw: String = "v41-runtime-image-build"
data vllm_image_build_receipt_path: String = "target/v41-runtime-image-build-receipt.txt"

fn vllm_image_receipt_line(b: VllmImageBuild) -> String {
  match b {
    VllmImageBuilt { identity: i, already_present: p } =>
      join([
        "image tag=", i.tag as String,
        " local_config_digest=", i.local_config_digest as String,
        " build_key=", serialize_content_hash(hash: i.build_key) as String,
        " requested_from=", i.requested_from.full_revision as String,
        " provenance=", vllm_image_provenance_wire(p: i.provenance) as String,
        " already_present=", if p { "true" } else { "false" }, "\n",
      ], "")
    VllmImageBuildRefused { step: st, cause: why } =>
      join(["image REFUSED step=", st as String, " cause=", why, "\n"], "")
  }
}

fn vllm_image_build_succeeded(b: VllmImageBuild) -> Bool {
  match b {
    VllmImageBuilt { identity: _, already_present: _ } => true
    VllmImageBuildRefused { step: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

func converge_vllm_runtime_image(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, ctx: SparkMaterializeContext, r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> VllmImageBuild
  uses net: Network
{
  if !vllm_recipe_arch_is_buildable(r: r) {
    VllmImageBuildRefused {
      step: "arch-admission" as NonEmptyStr,
      cause: join([vllm_torch_cuda_arch_list_wire(archs: r.torch_cuda_arch_list), " is not within vLLM's CUDA_SUPPORTED_ARCHS at the pinned revision, so the build would reject it"], ""),
    }
  } else {
    match converge_vllm_source(target: target, context: context, r: r) {
      VllmSourceRefused { step: st, cause: why } => VllmImageBuildRefused { step: st, cause: why }
      VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: _, already_present: _ } => converge_vllm_image(ctx: ctx, r: r)
    }
  }
}

// THE BUILD IS ITS OWN ENTRY POINT, SEPARATE FROM THE PROBE, for the reason the canary's cutover is
// separate from its convergence: this one can occupy most of a host's spare capacity for hours, and
// an entry that did both would put that cost behind every attempt to re-read a capability.
// ── THE FALLBACK BUILD, AND WHAT IS UNRESOLVED ABOUT RUNNING IT ──────────────────────────────
//
// THE DOCKER LEGS NOW GO WHERE DOCKER CAN BE REACHED, which changes where this entry can run at all.
// An earlier revision took `--arg host=` and drove docker over the key session as the managed
// executor -- an account in no docker group on any Spark -- so it could only ever have worked on a
// host carrying provisioning drift, and that is precisely how it did work: srv5 has a hand-installed
// key for the human briansrls and that account in the docker group, which no modeled route produces.
// The fallback image exists because of an unaccounted-for state.
//
// 🟡 dissolve-on: vllm_runtime_image_build_ci_wet -- THE FALLBACK BUILD HAS NOT BEEN EXECUTED THROUGH
// THIS TRANSPORT. The docker legs are now the modeled privileged route, which means the entry needs
// the administrator credential and therefore the fleet-converge boundary, the same as the probe. What
// is NOT settled is whether a build fits there: gunbc_ci_spark_serving_step_timeout_minutes is 60 and
// the one executed build of this image took about 75 minutes cold, so a from-scratch run would be cut
// off mid-compile. DISSOLVES WHEN either a step timeout that admits a cold build is agreed (a job-level
// decision, not this lane's), or this entry is executed against a warm context and its receipt lands
// here. Until then the fallback build is correct by reading on this transport and executed only on the
// other one, and this row is what keeps that from being read the other way round.
func vllm_runtime_image_build_ci_wet() -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  let now = clock_now_probed_at()
  match spark_converge_target_selection(execute_apply: true) {
    SparkConvergeTargetRefused { reason: why } => exit_failure(reason: why)
    SparkConvergeTargetAllObserveOnly =>
      exit_failure(reason: "SPARK_CONVERGE_TARGET is unset: a build occupies one host for the better part of an hour, so it refuses rather than choosing one")
    SparkConvergeTargetSingle { host: build_host } =>
      match admit_uncommitted_host(raw: build_host as String, purpose: vllm_build_host_purpose) {
        SparkHostRefused { cause: why } => exit_failure(reason: why as String)
        SparkHostAdmitted { host: _ } =>
        match observe_executor_reach() {
          ExecutorReachUnknown { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: join(["vllm_runtime_image_build_ci: ", c], ""))
          ExecutorReachKnown { short_hostname: executor, path: path } =>
            match read_bootstrap_credential() {
              BootstrapCredentialNotMaterialized { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: c as String)
              BootstrapCredentialReady { secret: administrator } =>
                match fleet_ssh_attempt_identity(raw: vllm_image_build_attempt_raw) {
                  Absent => exit_failure(reason: "attempt identity is not a safe path segment")
                  Present { value: attempt } =>
                    match probe_agent_credential_verification(attempt: attempt) {
                      FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed } =>
                        exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed }))
                      FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause } =>
                        exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause }))
                      FleetSshCredentialVerified { binding: binding } => {
                        let materialization = materialize_fleet_known_hosts_anchor(attempt: attempt)
                        match fleet_ssh_execution_context_of(outcome: materialization, credential: binding) {
                          Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["trust could not be established, so no leg ran: ", known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization)], ""))
                          Present { value: context } =>
                            match fleet_openssh_trust_policy_of(outcome: materialization) {
                              Absent => exit_failure(reason: "the trust policy could not be sealed for the privileged legs")
                              Present { value: trust } =>
                                match spark_reach_endpoint(host: build_host, path: ReachManagementLan) {
                                  Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["no ", spark_reach_path_wire(p: ReachManagementLan), " endpoint for ", build_host as String], ""))
                                  Present { value: endpoint } =>
                                    match with_materialized_ssh_key_file(
                                      identity_file_name: "gunbc-vllm-image-build-admin-password" as NonEmptyStr,
                                      secret_bytes: administrator as Secret,
                                      use: fn(file) {
                                        converge_vllm_runtime_image(
                                          target: SshTarget { endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, principal: spark_managed_executor_login as NonEmptyStr },
                                          context: context,
                                          ctx: SparkMaterializeContext {
                                            host: build_host,
                                            trust: trust,
                                            path: path,
                                            admin_credential: administrator as Secret,
                                            admin_credential_file: file,
                                            executor: HostCli,
                                          },
                                          r: v41_gb10_recipe(),
                                        )
                                      },
                                    ) {
                                      MaterializedSshKeyFileBracketRefused { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: c as String)
                                      MaterializedSshKeyFileBracketCompleted { value: build } => {
                                        let body = join([
                                          join(["vllm runtime image build probed_at=", probed_at_word(reading: now) as String, " host=", build_host as String, " endpoint=", endpoint, " executor=", executor, "\n"], ""),
                                          known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization),
                                          join(["recipe arch_list=", vllm_torch_cuda_arch_list_wire(archs: v41_gb10_recipe().torch_cuda_arch_list), " cuda=", v41_gb10_recipe().cuda_version as String, " base=", v41_gb10_recipe().build_base_image as String, "\n"], ""),
                                          vllm_image_receipt_line(b: build),
                                        ], "")
                                        let w = Filesystem.Write(path: vllm_image_build_receipt_path, content: body)
                                        if !w.success {
                                          exit_failure(reason: join(["vllm runtime image build receipt write failed: ", w.error], ""))
                                        } else if vllm_image_build_succeeded(b: build) {
                                          ExitSuccess
                                        } else {
                                          exit_failure(reason: join(["vllm runtime image build did not converge:\n", body], ""))
                                        }
                                      }
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                      }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
      }
  }
}

// ── ACQUIRING THE SOURCE ON ITS OWN ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE FIRST PHASE OF THE BUILD, ENTERED SEPARATELY, because it is the phase that had never executed:
// every modeled run so far found the tree already present and returned without cloning anything, so
// the clone leg was correct by reading and not by execution -- and it was not correct, since the
// repository IDENTITY it was handed is not a locator git can resolve. Exercising it costs a shallow
// clone rather than the hours a full build costs, and it is the same `converge_vllm_source` the build
// entry calls: this is not a second path, it is the first one entered at its own door.
data vllm_source_acquire_attempt_raw: String = "vllm-source-acquire"
data vllm_source_acquire_receipt_path: String = "target/vllm-source-acquire-receipt.txt"

fn vllm_source_state_line(s: VllmSourceState) -> String {
  match s {
    VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: rev, already_present: p } =>
      join(["source revision=", rev as String, " already_present=", if p { "true" } else { "false" }, " clean=true\n"], "")
    VllmSourceRefused { step: st, cause: why } =>
      join(["source REFUSED step=", st as String, " cause=", why, "\n"], "")
  }
}

fn vllm_source_acquired(s: VllmSourceState) -> Bool {
  match s {
    VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: _, already_present: _ } => true
    VllmSourceRefused { step: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

func vllm_source_acquire_wet(host: String) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  let now = clock_now_probed_at()
  match admit_uncommitted_host(raw: host, purpose: vllm_build_host_purpose) {
    SparkHostRefused { cause: why } => exit_failure(reason: why as String)
    SparkHostAdmitted { host: build_host } =>
      match fleet_ssh_attempt_identity(raw: vllm_source_acquire_attempt_raw) {
        Absent => exit_failure(reason: "attempt identity is not a safe path segment")
        Present { value: attempt } =>
          match probe_agent_credential_verification(attempt: attempt) {
            FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed } =>
              exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed }))
            FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause } =>
              exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause }))
            FleetSshCredentialVerified { binding: binding } => {
              let materialization = materialize_fleet_known_hosts_anchor(attempt: attempt)
              match fleet_ssh_execution_context_of(outcome: materialization, credential: binding) {
                Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["trust could not be established, so no leg ran: ", known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization)], ""))
                Present { value: context } =>
                  match spark_reach_endpoint(host: build_host, path: ReachManagementLan) {
                    Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["no ", spark_reach_path_wire(p: ReachManagementLan), " endpoint for ", build_host as String], ""))
                    Present { value: endpoint } => {
                      let target = SshTarget { endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, principal: spark_managed_executor_login as NonEmptyStr }
                      let state = converge_vllm_source(target: target, context: context, r: v41_gb10_recipe())
                      let body = join([
                        join(["vllm source acquire probed_at=", probed_at_word(reading: now) as String, " host=", build_host as String, " endpoint=", endpoint, " principal=", spark_managed_executor_login, "\n"], ""),
                        join(["root=", vllm_build_root() as String, " source=", vllm_source_dir() as String, " clone_url=", vllm_clone_url(source: v41_gb10_recipe().source), "\n"], ""),
                        known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization),
                        vllm_source_state_line(s: state),
                      ], "")
                      let w = Filesystem.Write(path: vllm_source_acquire_receipt_path, content: body)
                      if !w.success {
                        exit_failure(reason: join(["vllm source acquire receipt write failed: ", w.error], ""))
                      } else if vllm_source_acquired(s: state) {
                        ExitSuccess
                      } else {
                        exit_failure(reason: join(["the source was not acquired at the pinned revision:\n", body], ""))
                      }
                    }
                  }
              }
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

// ── WHO INVOKES THE ENTRIES IN THIS MODULE (DESIGN §3c) ───────────────────────────────────────
//
// NONE OF THEM HAS AN IN-TREE CALLER, AND THAT IS THE SHAPE OF AN ACTUATOR RATHER THAN A GAP -- but
// only if the invocation is named, which is what this block does. An entry whose consumer is "someone
// will type it" is dangling; an entry whose consumer is a stated dispatch is not.
//
// vllm_source_acquire_wet and spark_build_host_reachability_wet are OPERATOR-INVOKED through the
// ordinary CLI -- `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry
// dag/gunbc/spark/vllm_runtime_image_build.dag --function <name> [--arg host=<identity>]` -- which is
// how every read-only fleet instrument here is reached, and both have been EXECUTED that way against
// srv9, with their receipts in this lane's record. They take the fleet key from an in-run agent and
// need no credential an authoring session cannot hold, which is why they are CLI entries rather than
// dispatch modes.
//
// vllm_runtime_image_build_ci_wet is DIFFERENT and is a declared frontier: it needs the administrator
// credential, so its only honest invocation is a fleet-converge mode beside spark_runtime_image_probe.
// That mode is not added here, for a job-level reason stated in the dissolve-on beside the entry.
// Until it exists the entry is reachable only by an operator who materializes the credential
// themselves, and this paragraph is the record that its dispatch is owed.

// ── CAN THIS LANE REACH THE HOST IT WAS GIVEN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A PRECONDITION OF BOTH ENTRIES, ASKED ON ITS OWN. Admissibility says a host is one this lane MAY
// use; it says nothing about whether the fleet key is installed on it or whether anything answers at
// its endpoint. Those are different failures with different remedies -- a policy answer, a
// provisioning answer -- and discovering the second in the middle of a build is how an hour gets
// spent before the first leg refuses.
//
// IT ASKS ONE QUESTION AND NOTHING ELSE: the remote host's own short hostname, over the same sealed
// transport every other leg uses. What comes back is the host's answer about its own identity, which
// is exactly the join a caller needs -- an endpoint resolved from a fleet identity should answer with
// that identity's machine, and a reachable box that answers with a different name is a fleet-data
// defect rather than a reachable host.
// THE JOIN IS PERFORMED, NOT DESCRIBED. An earlier revision captured `reported_hostname` and compared
// it to nothing, while the annotation above claimed the comparison as the point of the probe -- a
// promise in prose that the code did not keep, which is worse than not making it.
//
// AND THE NAME IT IS COMPARED TO IS NOT THE IDENTITY. A Spark answers `hostname -s` with its VENDOR
// LABEL (spark-a3ee), never with its fleet identity (srv5); asserting equality against the identity
// would call every host in the fleet a defect. The label bound to an identity is recorded by
// gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_observed as `spark_hostname` on that host's lane observation, so the join
// is between two independently obtained facts: a lane reading taken earlier, and the box's own answer
// now. A host with no lane row cannot be joined at all, which is its own arm rather than a pass.
type SparkHostReachability
  = SparkHostAnswered { host: HostIdentity, endpoint: NonEmptyStr, reported_hostname: NonEmptyStr }
  | SparkHostIdentityMismatch { host: HostIdentity, endpoint: NonEmptyStr, expected: NonEmptyStr, reported: NonEmptyStr }
  | SparkHostIdentityUnjoinable { host: HostIdentity, endpoint: NonEmptyStr, reported: NonEmptyStr }
  | SparkHostUnreachable { host: HostIdentity, endpoint: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }
  | SparkHostUnresolved { host: HostIdentity }

fn spark_expected_reported_hostname(host: HostIdentity) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match fabric_lane_for_host(host: host) {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: o } => Present { value: o.spark_hostname }
  }
}

fn classify_reported_hostname(host: HostIdentity, endpoint: NonEmptyStr, reported: NonEmptyStr) -> SparkHostReachability {
  match spark_expected_reported_hostname(host: host) {
    Absent => SparkHostIdentityUnjoinable { host: host, endpoint: endpoint, reported: reported }
    Present { value: expected } =>
      if (expected as String) == (reported as String) {
        SparkHostAnswered { host: host, endpoint: endpoint, reported_hostname: reported }
      } else {
        SparkHostIdentityMismatch { host: host, endpoint: endpoint, expected: expected, reported: reported }
      }
  }
}

func probe_spark_host_reachability(context: FleetSshExecutionContext, host: HostIdentity) -> SparkHostReachability
  uses net: Network
{
  match spark_reach_endpoint(host: host, path: ReachManagementLan) {
    Absent => SparkHostUnresolved { host: host }
    Present { value: endpoint } => {
      let target = SshTarget { endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, principal: spark_managed_executor_login as NonEmptyStr }
      match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: "reachability" as NonEmptyStr, command: hostname_short_read_command()) {
        ImageLegFailed { step: _, cause: why } => SparkHostUnreachable { host: host, endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, cause: why }
        ImageLegRan { stdout: name } =>
          if name == "" {
            SparkHostUnreachable { host: host, endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, cause: "the leg succeeded and the host reported no name" }
          } else {
            classify_reported_hostname(host: host, endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, reported: name as NonEmptyStr)
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn spark_host_answered(r: SparkHostReachability) -> Bool {
  match r {
    SparkHostAnswered { host: _, endpoint: _, reported_hostname: _ } => true
    SparkHostIdentityMismatch { host: _, endpoint: _, expected: _, reported: _ } => false
    SparkHostIdentityUnjoinable { host: _, endpoint: _, reported: _ } => false
    SparkHostUnreachable { host: _, endpoint: _, cause: _ } => false
    SparkHostUnresolved { host: _ } => false
  }
}

fn spark_reachability_line(r: SparkHostReachability) -> String {
  match r {
    SparkHostAnswered { host: h, endpoint: e, reported_hostname: name } =>
      join(["reachable host=", h as String, " endpoint=", e as String, " reported=", name as String, " fleet_key=admitted\n"], "")
    SparkHostIdentityMismatch { host: h, endpoint: e, expected: want, reported: got } =>
      join(["IDENTITY MISMATCH host=", h as String, " endpoint=", e as String, " expected=", want as String, " reported=", got as String, " -- the endpoint answered, as a different machine than the fleet model binds to this identity\n"], "")
    SparkHostIdentityUnjoinable { host: h, endpoint: e, reported: got } =>
      join(["unjoinable host=", h as String, " endpoint=", e as String, " reported=", got as String, " -- no lane observation records a hostname for this identity, so the answer cannot be checked against anything\n"], "")
    SparkHostUnreachable { host: h, endpoint: e, cause: why } =>
      join(["unreachable host=", h as String, " endpoint=", e as String, " cause=", why, "\n"], "")
    SparkHostUnresolved { host: h } =>
      join(["unresolved host=", h as String, " -- no management-LAN endpoint\n"], "")
  }
}

data spark_reachability_attempt_raw: String = "spark-build-host-reachability"
data spark_reachability_receipt_path: String = "target/spark-build-host-reachability-receipt.txt"

// THE WHOLE ADMISSIBLE ROSTER IN ONE RUN, because the question a caller actually has is "which of
// these may I be given", not "is this one alive".
// A RECEIPT THAT WROTE IS NOT A LINE THAT RAN. An earlier revision of this entry returned success
// whenever the receipt file landed, so the run in which every admissible host refused the fleet key
// -- which is what actually happened -- exited zero, and any caller gating on it would have proceeded.
// The diagnostics were typed and located and then discarded by the exit path, which is §5's failure
// arm widening into silence. It now succeeds only when every admissible host answered AND its answer
// joined to the identity it was asked about.
func spark_build_host_reachability_wet() -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  let now = clock_now_probed_at()
  match fleet_ssh_attempt_identity(raw: spark_reachability_attempt_raw) {
    Absent => exit_failure(reason: "attempt identity is not a safe path segment")
    Present { value: attempt } =>
      match probe_agent_credential_verification(attempt: attempt) {
        FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed } =>
          exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed }))
        FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause } =>
          exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause }))
        FleetSshCredentialVerified { binding: binding } => {
          let materialization = materialize_fleet_known_hosts_anchor(attempt: attempt)
          match fleet_ssh_execution_context_of(outcome: materialization, credential: binding) {
            Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["trust could not be established, so no leg ran: ", known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization)], ""))
            Present { value: context } => {
              let readings = map(spark_uncommitted_hosts, h => probe_spark_host_reachability(context: context, host: h))
              let body = join([
                join(["spark build-host reachability probed_at=", probed_at_word(reading: now) as String, " question=short hostname only\n"], ""),
                join(["credential ", fleet_ssh_credential_locus_label(binding: binding), " public_material_identity=", serialize_content_hash(hash: fleet_ssh_credential_identity(binding: binding)) as String, "\n"], ""),
                join(map(readings, r => spark_reachability_line(r: r)), ""),
              ], "")
              let w = Filesystem.Write(path: spark_reachability_receipt_path, content: body)
              if !w.success {
                exit_failure(reason: join(["spark reachability receipt write failed: ", w.error], ""))
              } else if fold(readings, init: true, f: (acc, r) => acc && spark_host_answered(r: r)) {
                ExitSuccess
              } else {
                exit_failure(reason: join(["not every admissible build host answered:\n", body], ""))
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
  }
}
